  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Chilly start this morning across southwest Montana and north\n  central Idaho with patchy valley fog.\n\n- Warming and drying through mid week, then wetter Thursday into\n  Friday.\n\nClear skies south of Highway 200 in western Montana and north-central \nIdaho allowed temperatures to drop near or below freezing early \nthis morning, leading to patchy frost and valley fog that may \nexpand through daybreak. North of Highway 200, cloud cover remains\nthicker, keeping temperatures slightly milder. Expect passing \nmid- to high-level clouds across the region today, with a few \nlight, scattered showers remaining confined near the Canadian \nborder.\n\nSunny skies and warmer temperatures arrive Wednesday as high \npressure briefly builds over western Montana and central Idaho.\n\nRain chances return Thursday into Friday as a slow-moving weather \nsystem pulls abundant monsoonal moisture into the Northern \nRockies. While widespread severe weather is not expected, \nafternoon heating will trigger scattered thunderstorms. Because \nthese storms will likely move slowly, the primary hazard will be \nintense downpours.\n\nHigh pressure builds into the Northern Rockies as the low departs \nSaturday afternoon, bringing warm and dry conditions through at \nleast early next week. Confidence remains high for dry weather \nthrough Monday, though approximately 30% of ensemble members show \na cooler, wetter pattern returning by mid-week.",
